When your phone joins a mast, two questions should be answered: is this SIM a genuine subscriber, and is this mast a genuine network? GSM (2G) answered only the first. The network challenged the SIM; the SIM never challenged the network.

3GPP's own 3G design document, [TS 33.120](https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_ts/133100_133199/133120/04.00.00_60/ts_133120v040000p.pdf) (2001), lists the GSM weaknesses that 3G set out to correct, including:

- "active attacks using a 'false BTS' are possible";
- "cipher keys and authentication data are transmitted in clear between and within networks";
- encryption stopped at the base station, leaving traffic in clear on microwave links behind it;
- "data integrity is not provided";
- "fraud and LI were not considered in the design phase" (LI is lawful interception, lesson 12).

The first item is the root of the IMSI catcher (next lesson).

